# says so: "what a handler catches is resolved to a real exception class ...
# Not a name comparison." The EXPRESSION axis right beside it asked whether the
# callee was spelled `json.something`:
#
#     isinstance(func.value, ast.Name) and func.value.id == "json"
#
# So two writings of one operation were invisible. Driven against the shipped
# scan at d8215c5: `import json as j` + `j.loads(p.read_text(...))` with NO
# handler at all -> CLEAN; `from json import loads` + `loads(...)` with no
# handler -> CLEAN; the plain spelling -> REPORTED. A module that aliases its
# import escapes the rule entirely, handler or not. And the alias is not a
# hypothetical spelling: `foundry_orchestrator.py` carries a LIVE
# `import json as _json`. It is used only for `dumps` today, so there is no
# live raise — but the next `_json.loads(path.read_text(...))` written in that
# function is invisible on the day it is written, which is the ST-003 property
# under test.
#
# THE FIX IS THE SAME MOVE THE HANDLER AXIS ALREADY MADE: the callee is
# resolved through the module's own namespace — builtins, the real module
# object when importable, its `import X as Y` statements, and now its
# `from X import Y` statements too — and asked whether it IS `json.load` or
# `json.loads`. Not what it is called. A callee this resolver cannot see whose
# last segment is a loader's name is treated as a member and carries its
# spelling into the report, which is the same contract the handler axis holds:
# an unrecognised member ANNOUNCES itself rather than quietly widening the gap.
#
# SECOND GAP, SAME RULE: a handler that catches everything and RE-RAISES its
# own type (`except Exception as e: raise Boom(...) from e`) was counted as
# COVERING the site, while the operation still crosses the MCP boundary — as
# something the caller has no handler for at all. A converting handler now
# contributes nothing to `caught` and names itself in the report.
#
# THIRD SHAPE, found while fixing the first two: the read and the decode need
# not share an expression. `text = p.read_text()` under `except
# FileNotFoundError`, then `json.loads(text)` under `except
# json.JSONDecodeError`, is one operation spelled apart — between them naming
# two exception types and covering neither of the read's. Each rung is now
# judged against the handlers that enclose IT. That widening found a live site
# a hand search had missed, in `scripts/validate_intent_coverage.py`, on top of
# the one D-141 named in `validate-test-observations.py`.
#
# THE SWEEP D-147 ASKS FOR, over the other scans in this file that matched a
